{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Remember the days of eternity.</b> After completing the introduction to the song, in which he declared that its intent is to proclaim the righteousness of El, exalted be He — that He is a faithful El, to bestow good upon those who do His will, and He blesses and does not withdraw it, and there is no injustice in His measure of justice against them — Moses proceeded to explain this by narrating the past and the future: first, to make known how great was the intent of El, blessed be He, to achieve this end for the entire human species in the days of eternity and in the years of generation after generation, and how, when this did not succeed, HaShem acted greatly to elevate Israel to the rank of perfection, as He will do in the latter days with the remnant whom He will call. Second, that He gave them a fitting place to serve Him in gladness and goodness of heart from abundance of all, yet they rebelled and repaid evil for good — and whoever frustrates His intent despite all this is certainly deserving of severe punishment. Third, that through the magnitude of their sin they fell into the snares of the wicked and were worthy of utter destruction, had not the desecration of HaShem's name prevented it. Fourth, He made known the cause on account of which there will be redemption for them in the latter days. Fifth, He made known the manner of redemption and the mercies of El, blessed be He, upon the remnant of His people. These are the sections of parashat Ha'azinu, which they of blessed memory recalled and gave as signs in them: הזיו לך (Rosh HaShanah 31a). He said: if so — remember the days of eternity, and you will understand the ways of His goodness and how much He intended to bestow good upon the human species as a whole, when you remember first how He bestowed good upon the first man at the beginning of the days of eternity and placed him in the Garden of Eden, yet he corrupted his condition." "<b>Understand the years of generation after generation.</b> And you will understand, second, how much good He bestowed upon the generations, and they corrupted it — until the Flood. And likewise, third, from the Flood until the Dispersion, and they too corrupted."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The acrostic הזי״ו לָ״ךְ (Rosh HaShanah 31a) marks the six sections of Ha'azinu; left in Hebrew as Sforno cites it. גֶּרֶם הַמַּעֲלוֹת rendered as 'rank of perfection' following Sforno's teleological vocabulary."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:23:01.027160134Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}